  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the continuous-time Fourier transform of a finite rectangular pulse by evaluating the transform integral over the pulse interval and converting the result into a scaled sinc spectrum with the correct amplitude, width, and phase.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the centered unit-height rectangular pulse $x(t)=1$ for $|t|\\le T/2$ and $0$ otherwise. For this base case, the continuous-time Fourier transform only needs the interval where the pulse is nonzero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\begin{cases}1, & |t|\\le T/2\\\\0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the pulse is centered, its transform is real and even. That lets us replace the infinite integration range with the support interval $[-T/2,\\,T/2]$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-T/2}^{T/2} e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluating the antiderivative and subtracting the endpoint values gives the sine-over-argument form. This is the same transform, now written in a way that makes the spectrum shape visible.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}=T\\,\\mathrm{sinc}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\omega T}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This centered pulse is the base case to remember. Later changes in amplitude, width, or time shift will reuse the same sinc envelope and adjust only the scale or phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Transform Integral for a Centered Unit-Height Pulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=1$ for $-T/2\\le t\\le T/2$ and $0$ otherwise, starting from the transform integral and simplifying the answer as far as possible.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a centered, unit-height rectangular pulse of width $T$, so its support is exactly $[-T/2,\\,T/2]$. Start from the CTFT definition and restrict the integral to that interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-T/2}^{T/2} e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate $e^{-j\\omega t}$ to get $\\frac{e^{-j\\omega t}}{-j\\omega}$, then evaluate the antiderivative at the two endpoints. That gives the exponential-difference form below.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\left.\\frac{e^{-j\\omega t}}{-j\\omega}\\right|_{-T/2}^{T/2}=\\frac{1}{-j\\omega}\\left(e^{-j\\omega T/2}-e^{j\\omega T/2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use Euler's relation to rewrite the exponential difference as a sine term. The centered pulse therefore produces a real, even spectrum with the compact sine-over-$\\omega$ form below.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "By continuity, the value at $\\omega=0$ is $X(0)=T$. This is the base transform shape that later amplitude, width, and shift changes will modify.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Converting the Centered Pulse Transform into Sinc Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $X(\\omega)=\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}$ for the centered rectangular pulse, rewrite the answer in sinc form and identify the equivalent compact expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The transform has already been found, so the cue here is the algebraic rewrite. Keep the same centered pulse notation and convert the sine-over-$\\omega$ expression into the standard sinc form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Factor out $T$ so the denominator becomes $\\omega T/2$ and the remaining ratio matches the standard sinc definition. This is the same spectrum, just written in the compact form that makes the pulse-width scaling explicit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=T\\,\\frac{\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega T/2}=T\\,\\mathrm{sinc}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\omega T}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "With $\\mathrm{sinc}(u)=\\frac{\\sin u}{u}$, the rewrite preserves the real, even spectrum from the previous step. At $\\omega=0$, the limit is still $X(0)=T$, so the compact form matches the original transform exactly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Including Amplitude and Width in the Scaled Sinc Transform",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=A$ for $-T/2\\le t\\le T/2$ and $0$ otherwise, and express the answer in scaled sinc form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same centered rectangular pulse as before, but now the pulse height is $A$ instead of $1$. Keep the same support interval and factor the amplitude out of the transform integral first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-T/2}^{T/2} A\\,e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The remaining integral is exactly the centered unit-height case, so the same sine-over-$\\omega$ structure appears. The only new job is to preserve the factor $A$ all the way through the calculation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=A\\,\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the result in sinc form exactly as before, now with the amplitude factor still attached. The width $T$ appears in the sinc argument and in the overall scale factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=AT\\,\\mathrm{sinc}\\!\\left(\\frac{\\omega T}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This shows the scaling rule clearly: multiplying the pulse by $A$ multiplies the spectrum by $A$, and changing the width $T$ changes both the height and the horizontal spread of the sinc envelope. The centered pulse is still the same base shape, just rescaled.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Rectangular Pulse",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Including the Phase Factor for an Off-Center Rectangular Pulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=1$ for $0\\le t\\le T$ and $0$ otherwise, and rewrite the result as a sinc spectrum with the correct linear phase factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the support interval $0\\le t\\le T$. This is the same width-$T$ rectangle as before, but shifted right by $T/2$, so the new feature is a linear phase factor rather than a new sinc envelope.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_0^T e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate over the nonzero interval and evaluate the endpoints. The result is the same exponential-difference form that appears for any shifted rectangle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\frac{1-e^{-j\\omega T}}{j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now factor the midpoint term $e^{-j\\omega T/2}$ so the phase and magnitude separate cleanly. That leaves the same sine-over-$\\omega$ structure as the centered pulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=e^{-j\\omega T/2}\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}=T\\,e^{-j\\omega T/2}\\,\\mathrm{sinc}\\left(\\frac{\\omega T}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The sinc magnitude is unchanged from the centered case. The only new factor is the linear phase ramp from shifting the pulse by $T/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
